# Bounded native group recycle candidate

This work-only candidate adds group list/preview and confirmed recursive recycle, permanent delete and previous-parent restoration. It is not installed in Studio. Entry/history/KDBX support and its earlier receipts remain separate epochs. No whole-product, full compatibility, security, timing or memory superiority claim is made.

`recycleGroupList`, `recycleGroupRead`, `recycleGroupDelete` and `recycleGroupRestore` use native UUIDs and a private-key HMAC over the complete current XML/attachment table plus the selected group UUID and a separate group token domain. A stale graph, changed target or token from another key/entry command refuses. The queued server captures caller-owned inputs before awaiting, stages a cloned native document and publishes session state only after the encrypted store accepts the new document. Preview includes group names/notes and descendant entry title, username and URL; it returns no entry passwords, notes, TOTP secrets or history contents.

The exact KeePass2.61.1 source distinguishes these cases: the root/virtual group cannot be deleted; an enabled ordinary group is moved intact under the recycle bin; an explicit permanent choice, disabled recycle setting, bin descendant, the bin itself, or an ancestor containing the bin permanently deletes the selected subtree. A permanent delete appends active entry UUID tombstones first, then child group tombstones in recursive postorder, then the selected group UUID. History records are part of their entry node, not separate active tombstones. Existing tombstones stay in order. A removed bin UUID may remain dangling in native metadata, as in the studied original path; a later recycle can create a new bin.

Recycle updates only the moved group's PreviousParentGroup, LocationChanged, LastAccessTime and uint64 UsageCount, and touches its new bin/ancestor chain. Descendant group/entry timestamps, entry history, UUIDs, XML fields and attachment references remain intact. Restore moves the complete node to a valid PreviousParentGroup, updates location/previous-parent, and performs no Touch. A missing, zero, current-parent, self, descendant, bin-internal or depth-incompatible previous parent refuses here. There is no automatic root fallback. The broader original Move to Previous Parent command, including moves into/within the bin, is outside this restore-only subset.

The UI requires the active entry editor to be closed with New entry and its draft saved or discarded before a group mutation. New typing remains enabled while a group response is held; entry writes/opening are refused until the group operation completes. A held older entry read cannot publish after group mutation starts. Group list/preview/mutation publications check current view and panel generations after each await, including the active-entry refresh; close/disposal prevents follow-up publication. This deliberately narrower editor policy is not claimed as original GUI behavior.

Local limits remain: 8MiB combined XML and attachment Base64 text, 10,000 active groups, 10,000 active entries, 10,000 tombstones, XML100,000 nodes/depth64, native group depth63 and projected group path256 characters. XML nesting and path limits can reject a shallower group move; the released PwGroup.MaxDepth is126. The candidate validates its final document again before success. Existing input12MiB/depth64/nodes100,000, KDBX8MiB, KDF, attachment/key-file and five-minute session limits remain. These are synchronous cooperative local bounds, not wall-time preemption or process memory isolation. The caller document, working XML tree, projected entries, serialized copies and final validation tree can coexist; attachment decoding and encryption/export allocate additional bounded copies. No peak-RAM cap is asserted.

The candidate exporter emits KDBX4.1. An actual released writer with a retained non-default QualityCheck=false feature writes4.1 and preserves PreviousParentGroup. Its default-feature save can downgrade to4.0, which drops group previous-parent on reopen; both representations are tested separately. The new-bin unused ExpiryTime uses this operation clock, unlike the original process-default timestamp; Expires=false. Unknown XML is preserved by the candidate. Original library projections compare only the named supported fields, not arbitrary unknown XML retention by the original writer.

Original verification authenticates exact KeePass2.61.1 assembly bytes before every owned bounded child and invokes real AddGroup, Touch, DeleteAllObjects, KDBX open/save and the private static bin/previous-parent helpers. Command orchestration is source-composed from MainForm_Functions; fixed UUID/clock assignments are explicitly counted fixture adapters. Original GUI/full program executions are zero. DOM-model tests run the staged real client/server against an owned encrypted in-memory store, not a real browser or disk crash test. Future installation, actual browser/HTTP host acceptance and independent review remain pending.

Remaining gaps include explicit destination selection/fallback, group create/edit/tag controls, multi-selection, whole-bin emptying, special-group-reference repair, general move commands, recursive searching/Auto-Type behavior, renderer/GUI comparison, conflict synchronization, process isolation and full documented feature coverage. The historical159 source fingerprints and232 capability IDs are unchanged.

Source spans and exact pins are in the stage `contract-map.json`, `source-provenance.json` and `authority-baseline.json`. Full selected original source and GPL text are retained in `source-study/selected`; see GROUP-RECYCLE-NOTICE.md.
